      "side": 1,
      "message": "Thanks for checking further.\n \nIMG texture hardware flow:\nMemory -\u003e TPU(texture process unit) -\u003e USC(shader compiler) -\u003e PBE(pixel buffer engine) -\u003e Memory\nSome old cores, it has TPU, but lacks PBE functions.\n\n- TPU reads from memory, and then fetching, decoding and filtering texture data based on shader instructsions\n  It applies to usage as : USAGE_GPU_SAMPLED_IMAGE/GPU_TEXTURE\n- PBE writes to memory, it does format conversions, swizzle, packing and output to memory.\n  It applies to usage as : AHARDWAREBUFFER_USAGE_GPU_FRAMEBUFFER/AHARDWAREBUFFER_USAGE_GPU_COLOR_OUTPUT/GPU_RENDER_TARGET\n\nThus, USAGE_GPU_SAMPLED_IMAGE is readible, it doesn\u0027t mean gpu can write to memory.\n\nI\u0027m not expert in JPEG. Some ideas for your reference:\n\nmethod A) \nUse non-yuv format instead. In VirtualCameraRenderThread::renderIntoEglFramebuffer(), it seems there has alternative method of using RGB to render.\n    const bool renderSuccess \u003d\n        isYuvFormat(static_cast\u003cPixelFormat\u003e(textureBuffer-\u003egetPixelFormat()))\n            ? mEglTextureYuvProgram-\u003edraw(...)\n            : mEglTextureRgbProgram-\u003edraw(...)\n\nmethod B)\n - Call gralloc API isSupported() before allocation, it may return failure to indicate it can\u0027t be allocated.\n - If YUV not supported, fallback to use other formats",
